= Chrysanthemum (菊花)

_Scope: this entry treats the flower-head of *Chrysanthemum × morifolium* Ramat. — the Chinese drug *菊花 (júhuā)* — together with its distinct sibling drug *野菊花 (yějúhuā)*, the wild chrysanthemum (*C. indicum* L.). Three disambiguations are load-bearing: (1) chrysanthemum is *not* chamomile — European *Matricaria* / *Chamaemelum* (camomila, *bābūnaj*) is an unrelated Asteraceae with its own Greco-Roman and Tibb tradition, treated in chamomile.typ, and the modern Chinese coinage *洋甘菊* for chamomile has produced a traceable two-way confusion flagged below; (2) the cultivated drug 菊花 and the wild 野菊花 are *separate drugs* with different strength and indications, not grades of one; (3) the culinary "chrysanthemum greens" of East Asian cooking are *茼蒿 (tónghāo)*, a different species eaten as a leaf vegetable, not this flower drug. Status: deeply classical in Chinese medicine — *Shennong Bencao Jing* superior grade (上品) — and one of the most culturally saturated plants in the Chinese literary tradition, with substantial parallel standing in Japan and Korea. Not classical in Ayurveda or in Tibb; those sections are reasoned extension, flagged as such, with a nomenclatural trap in the Arabic register noted explicitly._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* *_Chrysanthemum × morifolium_ Ramat.*, family Asteraceae (Compositae) — the cultivated medicinal and ornamental chrysanthemum, a *complex ancient hybrid cultigen* rather than a clean wild species, its parentage (involving _C. indicum_ and related taxa) obscured by some fifteen centuries of Chinese selection. *_Chrysanthemum indicum_ L.* — *wild chrysanthemum*, the source of the separate drug *野菊花*. The genus has been shuffled repeatedly between _Chrysanthemum_, _Dendranthema_, and back, and both _Dendranthema morifolium_ and _Chrysanthemum morifolium_ appear in the pharmacognostic literature; current usage favours _Chrysanthemum_.#footnote[Taxonomic history (the _Chrysanthemum_/_Dendranthema_ oscillation, resolved in favour of _Chrysanthemum_ for the cultivated chrysanthemum by nomenclatural ruling) and the hybrid-cultigen status of _C. × morifolium_: standard horticultural-taxonomic references; the two drugs 菊花 (_C. × morifolium_) and 野菊花 (_C. indicum_) are monographed separately in the _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ and treated as distinct in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Genus etymology:* *Chrysanthemum* is Linnaean (1753), from Greek *χρυσός (chrysós,* "gold") + *ἄνθεμον (ánthemon,* "flower") — "gold-flower." *A flagged Linnaean false friend:* Linnaeus coined the name for *Mediterranean* annual species (the "crown daisy" group, now generally _Glebionis coronaria_), not for the East Asian cultivated chrysanthemum, which he never treated; the name was extended eastward by later botanists. The "gold" is therefore a Mediterranean yellow-daisy observation retro-fitted onto a Chinese drug whose premium medicinal grades are mostly *white*. This is the same class of nomenclatural accident as _lýkion_/_Lycium_ in `goji-berry.typ` and _kóïx_/_Coix_ in `coix.typ`.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. χρυσός, ἄνθεμον); Linnaeus, _Species Plantarum_ (1753), where the genus is founded on Mediterranean material. The eastward extension of the name to the Chinese cultigen is later. Flagged so the Greek "gold-flower" is not read as a description of the Chinese drug.]

*English:* *chrysanthemum*, a direct scientific-Latin loan entering general English in the 18th–19th c. as Chinese and Japanese cultivars reached Europe (the Victorian chrysanthemum craze); colloquially *"mum."* *Flagged*: ornamental "mums" bred in the modern West for form and colour are *not* the pharmacopoeial drug — the medicinal cultivars are a distinct, named, regionally-specified set (below).

*Chinese:* *菊 / 菊花 (jú / júhuā)* — among the oldest continuously attested plant-names in Chinese civilization. The drug appears in the *神農本草經 (Shennong Bencao Jing)*, Han period, in the *上品 (superior grade)* — the longevity-and-constitution class — under the archaic orthography *鞠華 / 鞠* in Han-period texts.#footnote[菊/鞠華 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經), 上品 class; the archaic 鞠 orthography is standard in Han pharmacological texts and discussed in modern Bencao textual scholarship. Li Shizhen's _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目, 1578) carries an extensive 菊花 entry that distinguishes cultivar types and discusses the wild/cultivated split.]

*The cultural register — unusually heavy for a drug.* Chrysanthemum is one of the *四君子 (sì jūnzǐ, "Four Gentlemen")* of literati painting and poetry — plum (梅), orchid (蘭), bamboo (竹), chrysanthemum (菊) — standing for *autumn, endurance, and reclusive integrity*, as the flower that blooms last and holds through frost. *Caution, and a real trap:* this cultural quartet is *entirely unrelated* to the Chinese-medicine formula *四君子湯 (Sì Jūnzǐ Tāng, "Four Gentlemen Decoction")* — ginseng, atractylodes, poria, honey-fried licorice, the foundational Spleen-qi tonic (see `fuling.typ`) — which borrows the same four-virtues trope for four herbs. The literary association was fixed by *陶淵明 (Tao Yuanming, 365–427)*, whose line *採菊東籬下，悠然見南山* ("picking chrysanthemums by the eastern hedge, in leisure I see the southern mountain") became the most-quoted chrysanthemum couplet in the language. The *重陽節 (Chóngyáng Jié,* Double Ninth Festival, 9th day of the 9th lunar month) is marked by chrysanthemum-viewing and *菊花酒 (júhuājiǔ,* chrysanthemum wine), a seasonal longevity preparation continuous with the drug's 上品 standing.#footnote[Tao Yuanming, "飲酒" ("Drinking Wine") no. 5; translations: Hightower, _The Poetry of T'ao Ch'ien_ (Oxford, 1970); Davis, _T'ao Yüan-ming_ (Cambridge, 1983). The 四君子 painting convention is a post-Tang literati development, fully formed by the Yuan-Ming period; the homonymy with 四君子湯 (Song, _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_, 1108) is coincidental and is flagged because it genuinely misleads readers.]

*Drug-grade cultivars — the real drug-form axis for this herb.* Chinese pharmacy specifies medicinal chrysanthemum by *named cultivar-and-origin*, in the same premium-origin logic as Ningxia goji (`goji-berry.typ`) or Yunnan poria (`fuling.typ`):
- *杭菊 / 杭白菊 (Hángjú / Háng Báijú)* — Zhejiang (Tongxiang/Hangzhou region); white-flowered; the grade most associated with the *eye-clearing and heat-clearing* uses and with everyday tea.
- *滁菊 (Chújú)* — Chuzhou, Anhui; white, downy; often ranked the finest for *Liver-calming*.
- *亳菊 (Bójú)* — Bozhou, Anhui (the great herb-trading city); white.
- *貢菊 / 徽菊 (Gòngjú / Huījú)* — Huangshan, Anhui; historically presented as *imperial tribute*, hence the name; favoured for eye complaints.
- *懷菊 (Huáijú)* — Henan, one of the 四大懷藥 ("four great Huai medicinals") region's products.
- *野菊花 (yějúhuā)* — _C. indicum_; *a separate drug*, more bitter and colder, for *heat-toxin* conditions (sores, boils), not a lesser grade of the above.

*Two further confusables, flagged:* *茼蒿 (tónghāo)* — "chrysanthemum greens," the leaf vegetable of _Glebionis coronaria_ (the very Mediterranean plant on which Linnaeus founded the genus name), eaten in Chinese, Japanese (_shungiku_), and Korean cooking — *a food, not this drug*. And *洋甘菊 (yáng gānjú)* — chamomile, whose 菊-containing modern coinage has caused chamomile to be assimilated to the cooling 菊花 register in modern Chinese popular sources (see `chamomile.typ`, where the artefact is treated from the other side).

*Japanese:* *菊 (kiku)*; the flower is the *imperial emblem* (菊花紋章, _kikka monshō_, the sixteen-petal chrysanthemum crest of the Imperial House, and the Supreme Order of the Chrysanthemum), and *菊の節句 (kiku no sekku)* is the Japanese Double-Ninth Chrysanthemum Festival. *食用菊 (shokuyōgiku)* — edible chrysanthemum petals, a garnish and vegetable in Japanese cuisine; *菊花茶 (kikkacha)* — the tea. The Japanese register is thus imperial-symbolic and culinary as much as medicinal, the pharmacological use being largely continuous with the Chinese.

*Korean:* *국화 (gukhwa)*; *국화차 (gukhwacha)*, chrysanthemum tea — a standard Korean traditional-tea (_cha_) preparation.

*Sanskrit:* *no classical name.* _C. × morifolium_ is an East Asian cultigen with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus; modern Indian horticultural usage borrows *सेवन्ती / शेवंती (sevantī / shevantī)* for garden chrysanthemum (a regional/Marathi-derived horticultural name, used for garland flowers), and the drug has no classical dravyaguna standing. Treated as reasoned-only below.#footnote[_Sevantī/shevantī_ is a modern Indian horticultural-and-garland name for cultivated chrysanthemum, not a classical dravyaguna lemma; Monier-Williams has no corresponding classical medicinal entry for the East Asian cultigen. Flagged so the existence of an Indian vernacular name is not mistaken for canonical attestation.]

*Arabic / Persian (Tibb) — a nomenclatural trap, flagged:* modern Arabic renders chrysanthemum *أُقْحُوان (uqḥuwān)* and Persian *گل داوودی (gol-e dāvūdī)*. *The classical Arabic أقحوان is not this plant.* In the medieval pharmacological corpus _uqḥuwān_ denotes a daisy-type Asteraceae generally identified with *chamomile or feverfew* (_Matricaria_/_Anthemis_/_Tanacetum parthenium_ class) — so citing Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār *sub voce uqḥuwān* as a Tibb attestation for Chinese 菊花 would be a *misattribution produced by modern nomenclature*, and this entry does not do so. The identification of classical _uqḥuwān_ deserves verification against a specific _al-Jāmiʿ_ edition; what is clear is that _C. × morifolium_, an East Asian cultigen, was outside medieval Arabic pharmacy's reach.#footnote[Classical أقحوان is generally identified with chamomile/feverfew-class Asteraceae in the medieval Arabic pharmacological corpus, not with the East Asian _Chrysanthemum_ cultigen to which modern Arabic applies the word; Lane, _An Arabic-English Lexicon_ (s.v. أقحوان), and Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_ — identification flagged for verification against a specific edition. The point is recorded because the modern name makes a spurious Tibb pedigree easy to assert.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

菊花 is *deeply classical*: a _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品 drug, elaborated through the Bencao tradition and central to the Wen Bing (warm-disease) formulary. Its unusual three-flavour attribution matches an unusually broad action range for a flower drug.

*Property (性 xìng):* Slightly cold (微寒).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛), sweet (甘), bitter (苦) — *three flavours*, where most drugs carry one or two; the pungent note carries the exterior-releasing action, the bitter the heat-clearing, the sweet the gentleness that makes it tolerable as a daily tea.

*Channels (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Liver (肝).

*Actions and indications:*
- *疏散風熱 (shū sàn fēng rè)* — disperses wind-heat: early-stage wind-heat exterior patterns with fever, headache, sore throat, cough, red eyes. The canonical formula is *桑菊飲 (Sāng Jú Yǐn,* Mulberry Leaf and Chrysanthemum Drink), from Wu Jutang's *溫病條辨 (Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn,* 1798) — a deliberately *light, short-cooked* formula (Wu warns against over-decocting, which would drive off the aromatic exterior-releasing action), for the mild wind-heat and early *溫燥* (warm-dryness) cough of early autumn.
- *平抑肝陽 / 平肝 (píng yì gān yáng / píng gān)* — calms and downbears rising Liver yang: the *肝陽上亢* pattern of headache, dizziness, tinnitus, irritability, flushed face. This is the classical seat of chrysanthemum's long association with what modern readers meet as hypertensive-type complaints, and the reason it appears in *天麻鉤藤飲* and similar Liver-calming formulas.
- *清肝明目 (qīng gān míng mù)* — clears the Liver and brightens the eyes: red, dry, painful, or blurred eyes, spots in the visual field. *This is the drug's signature action*, and the one for which the white Hangzhou and Huangshan grades are specified. The canonical formula is *杞菊地黃丸 (Qǐ Jú Dì Huáng Wán,* Lycium-Chrysanthemum Rehmannia Pill) — *六味地黃丸* (Qian Yi, *小兒藥證直訣*, 1119; see `rehmannia.typ`, `fuling.typ`) plus goji berry and chrysanthemum — for *Liver-Kidney yin vacuity presenting in the eyes*, a formula that braids together three entries of this book (`goji-berry.typ`, `rehmannia.typ`, this one).
- *清熱解毒 (qīng rè jiě dú)* — clears heat and resolves toxicity: principally the action of *野菊花*, internally and topically for *癰腫瘡毒* (sores, boils, cellulitic swellings), often with 金銀花 (honeysuckle) and 蒲公英 (dandelion). Substantially colder and more bitter than the cultivated flower; *not interchangeable with it*.
- *Folk-dietetic register:* *菊花茶 (júhuā chá)* as an everyday cooling tea, especially in the south; *枸杞菊花茶* (with goji) is among the most ubiquitous household blends in China; chrysanthemum also enters the Cantonese *涼茶 (liángchá)* cooling-tea tradition and the folk category of *上火 (shànghuǒ,* "rising fire" — the everyday heatiness complaint of sore throat, mouth ulcers, red eyes, constipation) for which it is a first reach.

*Cautions:* cold-pattern presentations — *脾胃虛寒 (Spleen-Stomach vacuity cold)* with loose stools, poor appetite, cold limbs — where the 微寒 property is counter-indicated; 野菊花 more so than 菊花, and more so in quantity or prolonged use. No classical 十八反/十九畏 incompatibility attaches to it; the drug's classical reputation is gentle enough for daily beverage use in appropriate constitutions.#footnote[菊花 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (上品); Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (1578), 菊花 and 野菊花; Wu Jutang, _Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_ (溫病條辨, 1798) for 桑菊飲, including Wu's instruction on light/short decoction of aromatic exterior-releasing formulas; Qian Yi, _小兒藥證直訣_ (1119) for the 六味地黃丸 base of 杞菊地黃丸. Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. The 上火 and 涼茶 categories are living folk-dietetic registers rather than classical textual ones — flagged as such.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Chrysanthemum × morifolium _is an East Asian cultigen with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus; the modern Indian_ sevantī _is a horticultural name, not a dravyaguna lemma (see Nomenclature). The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only East-Asian entries (`green-tea.typ`, `fuling.typ`)._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — the heat-clearing note), Kaṭu (कटु, pungent — the aromatic exterior-releasing note), Madhura (मधुर, sweet — the gentle, daily-tolerable edge). The reasoned set maps directly onto the classical Chinese three-flavour attribution 辛甘苦, a convergence worth noting since the two frameworks arrive at it independently.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle — the penetrating aromatic quality), Sugandhi (सुगन्धि, fragrant).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — a well-grounded assignment, converging with the Chinese 微寒 and with the drug's entire indication-set (red eyes, heat-headache, sores). *This is the clean contrast with chamomile*, whose classical Galenic-Tibb reading is *Hot* (see `chamomile.typ`): the two superficially similar "daisy teas" sit on *opposite sides of the thermal axis* in their own home traditions, which is the strongest single argument against conflating them.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically* — there being no classical entry. If one were singled out, the candidate is the *eye-specific action* (_cakṣuṣya_ at a degree not predictable from a light, cool, bitter-pungent flower): the Chinese tradition treats 明目 as chrysanthemum's defining capacity rather than as one cooling effect among many, and a prabhāva-level reading is the natural dravyaguna rendering of that emphasis. Flagged as *interpretive*, not classical.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pitta-śāmaka (the governing effect — śīta-tikta); Kapha-śāmaka mildly (laghu-rūkṣa-kaṭu); *Vāta-vardhaka in excess* (laghu-rūkṣa-śīta is the Vāta-aggravating combination) — which is the dravyaguna restatement of the Chinese caution against use in cold-vacuity constitutions.

*Reasoned karma:*
- _Cakṣuṣya_ — eye-benefiting (the signature action; 清肝明目).
- _Pitta-śāmaka, dāha-praśamana_ — Pitta-pacifying, relieving burning and inflammation.
- _Śiraḥ-śūla-hara_ — relieving headache, specifically of heat/Pitta type (平肝 register).
- _Jvara-hara_ (mild) — antipyretic in the early/superficial stage (疏散風熱 register).
- _Viṣaghna, kuṣṭhaghna, kaṇḍūghna_ — anti-toxin, anti-dermatosis, antipruritic: the reasoned reading of *野菊花*'s 清熱解毒 use for sores and boils, internal and topical.
- _Manaḥ-praśamana_ (mild) — mind-settling, by way of the Liver-calming action rather than as a primary sedative.#footnote[Reasoned placement anchors on the classical Chinese 性味歸經 and indication-set, rendered into dravyaguna axes; it is *not* a classical Ayurvedic entry — flagged. The śīta assignment and its deliberate contrast with chamomile's Hot classical placement is the entry's central cross-system point. _Śiraḥ-śūla-hara_ is added to the glossary with this entry.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for C. × morifolium — extensions of conditional rules plus modern pharmacological cautions; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* _Doṣa-viruddha_ in Vāta excess and in cold-vacuity states (the laghu-rūkṣa-śīta profile); _kāla-viruddha_ in deep winter for cold constitutions; _agni-viruddha_ where _agni_ is weak (_manda-agni_), the cooling flower further damping digestion — the dravyaguna restatement of the classical 脾胃虛寒 caution.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* the Cantonese *涼茶 / 上火* register treats chrysanthemum as a first-line cooling tea and pairs it with goji (warming-neutral, Liver-Kidney tonifying) — the pairing functions as a folk corrective preventing the cooling tea from being purely draining; noted as (F)-tier living practice.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Asteraceae (Compositae) cross-allergy* — chrysanthemum is a *well-documented contact allergen*, with occupational contact dermatitis in florists and nursery workers among the better-characterized plant dermatoses; sesquiterpene lactones are the usual sensitizers. Individuals sensitized to ragweed, mugwort, or chamomile may cross-react. *This is a more substantial caution for chrysanthemum than for most tea-herbs in this book.*
  - *Pyrethrum relation* — the insecticidal pyrethrins come from the closely related *_Tanacetum cinerariifolium_* (Dalmatian pyrethrum, formerly _Chrysanthemum cinerariifolium_); *the tea chrysanthemum is not a pyrethrin source and is not insecticidal*, but the generic kinship explains both the shared sensitization potential and the frequent popular confusion of the two — flagged.
  - *Additive hypotensive effect* — theoretical, on the Liver-calming/vasorelaxant signal (below); relevant only to high intake alongside antihypertensive therapy, and not demonstrated clinically.
  - *Cold-pattern / hypothyroid-type presentations* — no pharmacological basis, but the classical caution is worth carrying across: prolonged heavy use of a cooling tea in a cold-vacuity constitution is the classical misuse pattern.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Chrysanthemum × morifolium _has no classical Tibb entry — an East Asian cultigen outside the geographic reach of medieval Arabic pharmacy, which received only a handful of East Asian materials (musk, camphor, cinnamon, rhubarb) through the Silk Road and sea trade. Crucially, the modern Arabic name *أقحوان (uqḥuwān)* must not be used to manufacture an attestation: in the classical corpus that word denotes a chamomile/feverfew-class Asteraceae, not this plant (see Nomenclature). The reading below is reasoned extension on the humoral axis, offered because the entry's template calls for it and because the placement is informative, not because any classical text supports it — flagged throughout._ See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Names:* modern Arabic *أُقْحُوان (uqḥuwān)* — *with the classical-reference caveat above*; modern Persian *گل داوودی (gol-e dāvūdī)*; no Unani-Hakeemi drug-name of classical standing.

*Reasoned mizāj:* *Cold and Dry, 1st–2nd degree* — the humoral rendering of 微寒 plus the light-dry aromatic character. *This is the exact inverse of chamomile's classical Hot-and-Dry 1st-degree placement*, and the inversion is the Tibb-side statement of the same point made in the Ayurveda section: the two "daisy teas" are thermal opposites in their home traditions.

*Reasoned afʿāl (actions):* *Mubarriḍ* (refrigerant — the governing action); *muqawwī al-baṣar* / *nāfiʿ li-l-ʿayn* (sight-strengthening, eye-benefiting — the humoral rendering of 明目, and the action for which a classical Tibb analogue would most plausibly have been sought); *jālī* (cleansing) and *muḥallil* (resolvent) for the topical sore-and-swelling use of the wild form; *musakkin li-l-ṣudāʿ* (headache-calming) of hot type; *mufarriḥ* in a mild degree.

*Reasoned istemālāt (indications):* *amrāḍ al-ṣafrā'* (bilious/hot-pattern conditions); hot-type headache and vertigo; inflamed and reddened eyes; hot swellings and sores (topical, the 野菊花 analogue); feverish catarrh of the hot type.

*Reasoned mawāneʿ (contraindications):* cold and phlegmatic (*balghamī*) temperaments and *amrāḍ al-balgham*, in which a Cold-Dry simple is contrary; cold-pattern digestive weakness.

*Reasoned muṣliḥ / badal:* by class convention a Cold-Dry simple is corrected with a warming aromatic or honey; the plausible *badal* within the actual Tibb pharmacopoeia would be the cooling flower simples — *banafsha* (violet) or *gul-e surkh* (red rose) for the eye-and-head register. *Explicitly reasoned, not textual*: no classical _muṣliḥ_/_badal_ table lists this drug, because the drug is not in the corpus.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none, and none possible* — the plant was unavailable in the Ḥijāz of the 7th century. Flagged because the ubiquity of chrysanthemum tea in the modern Muslim world (and the Arabic name) could otherwise invite a spurious devotional pedigree, exactly the error flagged for sage's _maryamiyya_ in `sage.typ`.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *桑菊飲 (Sāng Jú Yǐn)* — mulberry leaf and chrysanthemum, with apricot kernel, platycodon, forsythia, mint, reed root, licorice; for mild wind-heat cough and early autumn warm-dryness. _Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_, 1798. Light, brief decoction is constitutive, not optional.
- *杞菊地黃丸 (Qǐ Jú Dì Huáng Wán)* — goji and chrysanthemum added to 六味地黃丸; Liver-Kidney yin vacuity with eye signs. See `goji-berry.typ`, `rehmannia.typ`.
- *菊花散 / 甘菊花類方* — the Song-and-later formularies carry several chrysanthemum-led powders for head-and-eye wind-heat; *天麻鉤藤飲* uses chrysanthemum within the Liver-yang-calming category.
- *野菊花* preparations — internal decoction and topical application (mashed fresh herb, washes) for sores and boils, typically with 金銀花, 蒲公英, 連翹.
- *Dietetic and seasonal:* *菊花茶* and *枸杞菊花茶* (daily cooling tea); *菊花酒* (Double Ninth longevity wine); chrysanthemum in Cantonese *涼茶* blends; chrysanthemum-and-rock-sugar infusions for *上火*.

=== Ayurveda

*No classical Ayurvedic formula* — post-classical, non-canonical plant (see above). Modern Indian herbal commerce sells chrysanthemum tea under English/Chinese names; where Ayurveda-adjacent products use it, they slot it into the _cakṣuṣya_ and _pitta-śāmaka_ registers by extension — reasoned, not classical.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*No classical Greco-Roman medical tradition for this plant* — and the genus name is, as flagged, a Mediterranean coinage later extended eastward, so the Greek word supplies no pedigree. Three European registers do exist and are *not* medical in the sense this book means, listed to keep the record straight:
- *Culinary:* the Mediterranean _Glebionis coronaria_ ("crown daisy") on which the genus name was founded is eaten as a green in Mediterranean and, as *茼蒿/shungiku*, in East Asian cooking.
- *Insecticidal:* *pyrethrum* — the Dalmatian _Tanacetum cinerariifolium_ (long called _Chrysanthemum cinerariifolium_) — is a major economic-botanical tradition of the eastern Adriatic and later Kenya, and the source of the pyrethrin insecticides. *A related genus, not this drug*; noted because the name-overlap routinely misleads.
- *Ornamental:* the 19th-c. European and American chrysanthemum craze, following Japanese and Chinese cultivar imports, produced the modern florist "mum" — horticultural, not pharmacological.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical preparation* — see the Tibb section above, including the *uqḥuwān* nomenclatural caveat. Modern Middle Eastern consumption of chrysanthemum tea is a contemporary import of the Chinese register.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Medicinal chrysanthemum is a *warm-temperate East Asian* cultigen, harvested in *autumn* — a seasonality that is pharmacologically load-bearing rather than incidental, since its principal classical indication (early-autumn wind-heat with incipient dryness, the *溫燥* of the Wen Bing schema) is the very season of its harvest. See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-temperate East Asian monsoon (Jiangnan and Huai — the cultivar heartland)

Köppen Cfa/Cwa: hot humid summers, cool damp winters, a sharply marked autumn. Zhejiang (杭菊), Anhui (滁菊, 亳菊, 貢菊), and Henan (懷菊) are the named-origin zones. Constitutionally the summer carries 濕熱 with Spleen and Heart loading; the autumn turn brings 燥 with *Lung* loading per the 六淫 correspondences (燥→肺). *Traditional preparation and cares.* The constitutive form is the *light infusion of whole dried flower-heads* — not decoction; the aromatic 疏散風熱 fraction is volatile and over-boiling destroys it, which is exactly Wu Jutang's instruction for 桑菊飲. The autumn-harvest, autumn-indication, autumn-festival (重陽) alignment is the cleanest season-drug correspondence in this book after the pear's (`pear.typ`).

=== Hot-humid South China and Southeast Asia (the 涼茶 register)

Köppen Cfa/Am: long hot humid season; external 濕熱; Pitta-Kapha in dravyaguna terms. Chrysanthemum here is not primarily a seasonal wind-heat drug but a *daily dietetic coolant* — the Cantonese *涼茶* and *上火* register, taken as chrysanthemum-and-rock-sugar infusion, in herbal-tea-shop blends, and as bottled chrysanthemum tea (one of the most widely sold soft drinks in southern China and Hong Kong). *Cares:* the cooling-draining character suits the climate's heat burden but is easy to overuse in cold-vacuity constitutions; the folk pairing with *goji* (`goji-berry.typ`) is the tradition's own corrective, adding a warming-neutral tonic to a cooling drainer.

=== Temperate Japan and Korea (the kiku / gukhwa register)

Köppen Cfa/Dfa. The pharmacological use broadly follows the Chinese; what is distinctive is the *imperial-symbolic* register (菊花紋章) and the *culinary* one (*食用菊*, edible petals as garnish and vegetable; chrysanthemum-petal dishes in Japanese autumn cuisine), alongside *菊花茶 / 국화차* as a seasonal tea. The autumn-festival alignment (菊の節句) parallels the Chinese 重陽.

=== Climates where chrysanthemum is not traditional

Hot-arid, cold-dry high-altitude, and cool-damp Atlantic zones have no indigenous medicinal-chrysanthemum tradition; the ornamental plant travels everywhere, but the *drug-grade cultivar-and-origin system* is East Asian and remains so. The indications travel; the named grades do not.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Two genuine transposition problems. First, *grade and identity*: the global market now sells "chrysanthemum tea" without cultivar or origin specification, and florist-grade or ornamental material is not the pharmacopoeial drug — a _sampad-viruddha_ (wrong-quality) rather than _deśa_ problem, and the one most likely to matter in practice outside East Asia. Second, *season and constitution*: a cooling, Pitta-pacifying, mildly Vāta-aggravating tea taken year-round — and especially through cold damp winters in cool-temperate climates, or daily by cold-vacuity constitutions — is _kāla-viruddha_ and _doṣa-viruddha_ by the classical reckoning, and precisely the misuse the Chinese 脾胃虛寒 caution names. The East Asian register that exported the drink (southern-Chinese daily *涼茶*) is climatically matched to a hot-humid zone; the drink has travelled, the climate has not. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Chrysanthemum flower is consumed as an infusion and in small medicinal doses; it is a *medicinal-culinary flower*, not a nutritive food, and no per-100 g macronutrient profile is meaningful (the edible-petal Japanese use is a minor vegetable contribution only).

*Key constituents:*
- *Flavonoids* — *luteolin* and *luteolin-7-O-glucoside*, *apigenin* and *apigenin-7-O-glucoside*, *acacetin* glycosides (acacetin being relatively characteristic of _Chrysanthemum_), *quercetin* glycosides. Note the overlap with chamomile's apigenin — *the two unrelated plants share their principal flavonoid*, arrived at independently, which is a real convergence and arguably part of why both are perceived as calming teas.
- *Phenolic acids* — *chlorogenic acid* and the *dicaffeoylquinic acids* (3,5-di-O-caffeoylquinic acid prominent); the main antioxidant fraction.
- *Essential oil* — *chrysanthenone*, *camphor*, *borneol*, *bornyl acetate*, *germacrene D*, with the composition differing markedly between _C. × morifolium_ and _C. indicum_ (the wild drug's oil being harsher, consistent with its stronger, more bitter classical character).
- *Sesquiterpene lactones* — the usual Compositae contact sensitizers; the pharmacological basis of the dermatitis caution.
- *Carotenoids* — including *lutein*-class xanthophylls in the yellow-flowered material. *A flagged convergence, not a proof:* lutein and zeaxanthin are the macular xanthophylls of modern ophthalmic nutrition, and chrysanthemum's classical signature action is *明目* (brightening the eyes). The convergence is genuinely interesting; it is *not* evidence that the classical action is explained by dietary lutein, since infusion doses deliver trace amounts and the premium medicinal grades are white rather than yellow. Recorded as suggestive, explicitly not as mechanism.#footnote[Phytochemistry (luteolin/apigenin/acacetin glycosides; chlorogenic and dicaffeoylquinic acids; chrysanthenone/camphor/borneol oil; sesquiterpene lactones; carotenoid fraction) and the _C. × morifolium_ vs. _C. indicum_ compositional contrast: standard phytochemistry reviews on _Chrysanthemum morifolium_ and _C. indicum_ — verify against current literature. The lutein/明目 convergence is flagged as suggestive only; infusion-dose carotenoid delivery is trace, and the premium eye-grade cultivars are white-flowered, which argues against the lutein reading as mechanism.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antioxidant* — robust in vitro, on the chlorogenic/dicaffeoylquinic and flavonoid fractions; the best-characterized activity, with the usual caveat that in vitro antioxidant capacity translates poorly to clinical endpoints.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — luteolin and apigenin show activity in standard models; consistent with both the 清熱 and the topical 解毒 registers.
- *Antimicrobial* — *野菊花* (_C. indicum_) extracts show the stronger activity, consistent with its classical reservation for *heat-toxin* sores and boils rather than for tea; modest in vitro evidence.
- *Vasorelaxant / blood-pressure signal* — animal and small human studies report modest effects, consistent with the classical *平肝陽* indication; *preliminary, not established antihypertensive therapy*, and flagged as such. The classical indication and the modern signal agree in direction, which is worth noting without inflating.
- *Eye-related endpoints* — despite the signature classical action, *direct clinical evidence for chrysanthemum on ocular outcomes is thin*; what exists is preclinical (anti-inflammatory effects relevant to ocular surface irritation) plus the carotenoid convergence flagged above. An honest statement of the gap matters here precisely because the classical claim is so emphatic.
- *Hepatoprotective, neuroprotective* — preclinical signals in the modern literature; preliminary.#footnote[Pharmacology: representative reviews on _Chrysanthemum morifolium_ and _C. indicum_ bioactivity (antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial, vasorelaxant, hepato-/neuroprotective) — verify against current systematic reviews. Evidence tier is modest-to-preliminary throughout, consistent with this book's standard caveat for herbal-tea pharmacology (cf. `green-tea.typ`, `oolong-tea.typ`). The thinness of direct ocular-outcome evidence against the emphatic classical 明目 claim is stated deliberately rather than elided.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* chrysanthemum has *no toxicological ceiling at tea dose* and no limiting toxic constituent (contrast sage/wormwood thujone); the operative limits are *allergy*, *constitutional suitability*, and *material grade*.
- *菊花 (cultivated), infusion or decoction:* *5–10 g* dried flowers per day (clinical range; up to ~15 g in some formulas); daily beverage use at the low end is traditional and unproblematic in suitable constitutions.
- *野菊花 (_C. indicum_):* *9–15 g* in decoction; *a medicinal, not a daily tea* — colder and more bitter, reserved for heat-toxin indications and short courses.
- *Topical:* washes and poultices of the wild form for sores, per the classical use.
- *Cautions:* *Asteraceae/Compositae sensitization* — the most substantial modern caution for this herb (occupational chrysanthemum dermatitis is well documented); *cold-pattern constitutions* (脾胃虛寒, loose stools, cold intolerance) should limit or avoid habitual use; theoretical additive effect with antihypertensives at high intake; pregnancy — no specific classical contraindication, and ordinary tea use is not flagged as a risk, but concentrated extracts lack safety data.
- *Material grade matters*: use food/pharmacopoeial-grade named material (杭菊, 貢菊, 滁菊, 亳菊), not ornamental florist flowers, which may carry pesticide residues and are not the drug.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dosing: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ monographs for 菊花 and 野菊花, and Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (clinical dose ranges and the cultivated/wild distinction). Compositae contact-dermatitis literature for the allergy caution. Reference thresholds requiring current verification, not medical advice.]

